; the reading of a formula is stable (doc/kurt-soundness.md §10.2): the sort of a binder is fixed
; when the fact is stored, and later lines can't change it -- an operator's place gets no sort after
; its first use, a sort declaration then is refused, and a block's result keeps the sort its
; constant got. Otherwise a premise `∀ x (P x)` over all objects, read again over the numbers,
; would make the rule stronger
load natural
const P, Q
prop Q
prop P
arity P 1
use (∀ x (P x)) ⇒ Q                 ; `x` over all objects: `P`'s place has no sort
use P 1
use P {1}                           ; (the place still takes every object)
expect "EvalError"
    real P 1                         ; a sort after the first use is refused
use ∀ n (n + 0 = n ⇒ P n)
let y
    y + 0 = y                       ; `y` a number
    P y
expect "ProofError"
    Q                               ; `∀ y (P y)` is about the numbers, the premise about all objects
